  questions: [
    { q: "The MOOD of a passage refers to:", o: ["The author's attitude toward the subject matter", "The emotional atmosphere the passage creates in the reader/listener", "The main purpose the writer intended when writing", "The formal or informal register of the writing"], a: 1, e: "Mood is the emotional atmosphere or feeling that the passage creates in the reader/listener — it is the effect on the audience. Tone, in contrast, is the author's attitude toward the subject. Mood words include: joyful, melancholic, tense, peaceful, angry.", h: "MOOD = emotional atmosphere in the READER (effect on audience). TONE = AUTHOR'S attitude toward subject. Different concepts.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "A complex sentence MUST contain:", o: ["Two independent clauses joined by a coordinating conjunction", "At least three clauses", "One main (independent) clause and at least one subordinate (dependent) clause", "Only simple words and short phrases"], a: 2, e: "A complex sentence contains one main clause (independent — can stand alone) joined to one or more subordinate clauses (dependent — introduced by a subordinating conjunction; cannot stand alone). This distinguishes complex sentences from compound sentences (two independent clauses) and simple sentences (one clause).", h: "Complex sentence = MAIN CLAUSE + SUBORDINATE CLAUSE (joined by subordinating conjunction). Main = stands alone. Subordinate = cannot stand alone.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "In the sentence 'She left before he arrived,' the subordinate clause is:", o: ["She left", "Before he arrived", "She left before", "He arrived"], a: 1, e: "'Before he arrived' is the subordinate clause — it begins with the subordinating conjunction 'before' and cannot stand alone as a complete sentence. 'She left' is the main clause — it can stand alone. Subordinate clauses are introduced by subordinating conjunctions.", h: "Subordinate clause: introduced by a SUBORDINATING CONJUNCTION, CANNOT stand alone. 'Before he arrived' = subordinate. 'She left' = main.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "A text describing a funeral with slow, heavy language and images of loss creates which MOOD?", o: ["Joyful", "Melancholic/Sad", "Tense/Suspenseful", "Peaceful/Serene"], a: 1, e: "Slow, heavy language combined with images of loss, death, and grief creates a melancholic or sad mood — the reader feels sorrow, heaviness, or grief. The language choices (diction) and imagery work together to produce this emotional atmosphere.", h: "Melancholic/sad mood: created by somber language, images of LOSS, DEATH, grief. Slow heavy language = sad atmosphere.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "Which of the following is a SUBORDINATING CONJUNCTION?", o: ["And", "But", "Although", "Yet"], a: 2, e: "'Although' is a subordinating conjunction — it introduces a subordinate (dependent) clause and shows a contrast: 'Although it rained, we played.' 'And,' 'but,' and 'yet' are COORDINATING conjunctions — they join two independent clauses of equal grammatical weight.", h: "Subordinating conjunctions (introduce dependent clauses): although, because, when, if, unless, since, while. Coordinating (join equals): and, but, or, yet.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "A news article written in objective, professional language with no personal opinions shows which TONE?", o: ["Sarcastic", "Formal/Official", "Sympathetic", "Persuasive"], a: 1, e: "News articles are typically written in a formal/official tone — impersonal, professional, factual, and following journalistic conventions. They present information without personal emotional investment. Sarcastic tone mocks. Sympathetic tone shows empathy. Persuasive tone argues.", h: "Formal/official tone: IMPERSONAL, PROFESSIONAL, factual, no personal emotions. News reporting = formal tone.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "The purpose of a recipe book is PRIMARILY to:", o: ["Persuade readers to buy food products", "Inform and entertain readers about food history", "Instruct readers on how to prepare food", "Describe the appearance of food in vivid detail"], a: 2, e: "A recipe book's primary purpose is to instruct — to give readers step-by-step directions on how to prepare food. Instruction/direction is one of the six main purposes of writing, along with informing, persuading, entertaining, describing, and expressing.", h: "Recipes = to INSTRUCT (give directions on HOW TO DO something). Other purposes: inform, persuade, entertain, describe, express.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "Which of these correctly uses a subordinating conjunction for CAUSE?", o: ["She studied hard, and she passed.", "Although she studied hard, she passed.", "She studied hard because she wanted to pass.", "She studied hard; therefore, she passed."], a: 2, e: "'Because' is a cause subordinating conjunction — it introduces a clause explaining WHY something happened: 'She studied hard because she wanted to pass.' 'And' is coordinating. 'Although' introduces contrast. 'Therefore' is a conjunctive adverb, not a subordinating conjunction.", h: "CAUSE subordinating conjunctions: because, since, as. 'She studied BECAUSE she wanted to pass.' Because introduces the REASON (subordinate clause).", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "When a subordinate clause comes BEFORE the main clause, the correct punctuation is:", o: ["No punctuation between them", "A semicolon", "A comma after the subordinate clause", "Brackets around the subordinate clause"], a: 2, e: "When a subordinate clause precedes the main clause, a comma is used after the subordinate clause: 'Although it rained, we continued playing.' When the subordinate clause follows the main clause, no comma is typically needed: 'We continued playing although it rained.'", h: "Subordinate clause FIRST = comma after it: 'Although it rained, we played.' Subordinate clause SECOND = no comma: 'We played although it rained.'", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "The TONE of a speech by a politician arguing vigorously for improved roads in Nigeria would be:", o: ["Melancholic", "Didactic/Instructive", "Persuasive/Argumentative", "Sarcastic"], a: 2, e: "A politician arguing for a policy would use a persuasive/argumentative tone — presenting evidence, making appeals, and working to convince the audience to support their position. This contrasts with didactic (teaching), melancholic (sad), or sarcastic (mocking) tones.", h: "Political speech arguing for a policy = PERSUASIVE/ARGUMENTATIVE tone. Presents evidence, makes appeals, aims to CONVINCE.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "Identify the subordinate clause in: 'He will succeed if he works hard.'", o: ["He will succeed", "If he works hard", "Will succeed if", "He works hard"], a: 1, e: "'If he works hard' is the subordinate clause — it begins with the subordinating conjunction 'if' (condition) and cannot stand alone. 'He will succeed' is the main clause that can stand alone. If he works hard → condition for the main clause outcome.", h: "'If he works hard' = subordinate (conditional) clause, begins with 'if,' CANNOT stand alone. 'He will succeed' = main clause, can stand alone.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "The purpose of a text that uses emotional language to convince readers to donate to flood victims is:", o: ["To inform readers about flooding", "To entertain with stories of survival", "To persuade readers to take a specific action (donate)", "To instruct readers on how to prevent flooding"], a: 2, e: "Using emotional language to motivate a specific action (donating) is a persuasive purpose. The text is designed to convince readers to do something — donate. This differs from informative (presenting facts) or instructive (explaining how to do something) purposes.", h: "Emotional language + call to action (donate) = PERSUASIVE PURPOSE. Aims to convince readers to ACT.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "Which sentence is a COMPLEX sentence?", o: ["She studied and she passed.", "She studied hard.", "Because she studied hard, she passed the examination.", "She studied; she passed."], a: 2, e: "'Because she studied hard, she passed the examination' is a complex sentence — it contains a subordinate clause ('Because she studied hard') and a main clause ('she passed the examination') joined by the subordinating conjunction 'because.' Options A and D are compound sentences. Option B is a simple sentence.", h: "Complex sentence: subordinating conjunction (because, although, when, if) + subordinate clause + main clause. 'BECAUSE she studied, she passed' = complex.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "A story written in a style that creates excitement and fear through narrow escapes and danger has which MOOD?", o: ["Peaceful/Serene", "Melancholic", "Joyful", "Tense/Suspenseful"], a: 3, e: "Excitement, fear, narrow escapes, and danger create a tense or suspenseful mood — the reader feels anxious, alert, and on edge. Writers create suspense through short sentences, urgent verbs, cliffhangers, and uncertainty about outcomes.", h: "Tense/suspenseful mood: created by danger, uncertainty, short urgent sentences, near-misses. Reader feels ANXIOUS and on edge.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "The subordinating conjunction 'so that' introduces a:", o: ["Time clause", "Cause clause", "Purpose clause", "Condition clause"], a: 2, e: "'So that' introduces a purpose clause — it explains the intention or goal behind the main action: 'She studied hard so that she could pass.' The subordinate clause answers 'For what purpose?' or 'With what intention?'", h: "'So that' = PURPOSE clause (why the action was done). 'She studied so THAT she could pass' = purpose (intention behind studying).", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "Tone is BEST defined as:", o: ["The emotional atmosphere created in the reader by the passage", "The writer's or speaker's attitude toward the subject matter or audience", "The overall purpose or intention of a text", "The mood experienced by the characters in a story"], a: 1, e: "Tone is the writer's or speaker's attitude toward the subject — whether they are formal, informal, sarcastic, sympathetic, angry, enthusiastic, etc. It reflects HOW the writer feels about what they are writing about. Mood is the effect on the reader; tone is the author's stance.", h: "TONE = AUTHOR'S ATTITUDE toward subject/audience. MOOD = emotional effect on READER. Tone is about the writer; mood is about the reader's experience.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "In revision of an informal letter, which is a COMMON ERROR students make?", o: ["Using the first name in the salutation", "Using contractions in the body", "Including the recipient's postal address (applicable only in formal letters)", "Writing only the writer's address at the top right"], a: 2, e: "Including the recipient's address is a FORMAL letter feature — it does NOT belong in an informal letter. Using first names in salutations, contractions in the body, and writing only the writer's address are all CORRECT features of informal letters.", h: "Informal letter ERROR: including RECIPIENT'S ADDRESS. That is a formal letter feature only. Informal = only writer's address (top right).", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "The sentence 'The child cried because she was hungry' is complex because:", o: ["It has two independent clauses", "It contains a subordinate clause ('because she was hungry') joined to a main clause by 'because'", "It has more than five words", "It uses a coordinating conjunction to join two ideas"], a: 1, e: "This sentence is complex because it contains: main clause ('The child cried' — stands alone) + subordinating conjunction ('because') + subordinate clause ('she was hungry' — cannot stand alone). The subordinating conjunction 'because' creates the dependent relationship.", h: "'Because she was hungry' = subordinate clause (cannot stand alone). 'The child cried' = main clause. Joined by BECAUSE = complex sentence.", yr: "SS1" }
  ,
    { q: "Which of the following sentences demonstrates INCORRECT use of a subordinating conjunction?", o: ["She passed because she studied hard.", "Although it rained, we continued the match.", "She studied hard, although she passed.", "I will go if you come with me."], a: 2, e: "'She studied hard, although she passed' is logically incorrect — 'although' introduces a contrast or unexpected circumstance, implying the main clause is surprising. It should be 'She passed ALTHOUGH she studied very little' (the passing was surprising given little study). 'She studied hard, although she passed' makes no logical sense.", h: "'Although' introduces UNEXPECTED/CONTRASTING circumstance. 'She studied hard, although she passed' = illogical (no contrast). 'She passed ALTHOUGH she barely studied' = logical.", yr: "SS1" },
    { q: "Which sentence correctly identifies the PURPOSE of a formal government notice?", o: ["To entertain government workers with interesting stories", "To express the writer's personal feelings about government policies", "To inform the public or specific recipients about an official matter, requirement, or decision", "To create a dramatic narrative about events in government"], a: 2, e: "A formal government notice has the purpose of INFORMING — conveying official information about requirements, decisions, events, or policies to specific recipients or the general public. It is objective, formal, and factual — not personal, entertaining, or narrative.", h: "Government notice PURPOSE = INFORM (official matters, requirements, decisions). Objective, formal, factual. Not personal opinion or entertainment.", yr: "SS1" }
  ]
